window.AUTH=window.AUTH||{};window.AUTH["20-601"]={"d":"20-601","id":"2021/20-601","n":"Cameron v. EMW Women’s Surgical Center","briefs":[{"k":"petitioner","label":"Brief of petitioner Daniel Cameron, Attorney General of Kentucky","date":"2021-06-14","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/20/20-601/181643/20210614143821138_20-601%20Petitioner%20Brief%20Merits.pdf","side":"pet","n":67},{"k":"respondent","label":"Brief of respondents EMW Women's Surgical Center, P.S.C., et al.","date":"2021-08-13","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/20/20-601/187422/20210813120705705_20-601%20Cameron%20v%20EMW%20Women%20Surgical%20Center%20Brief%20for%20Respondents.pdf","side":"res","n":91},{"k":"reply","label":"Reply of petitioner Daniel Cameron, Attorney General of Kentucky","date":"2021-09-13","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/20/20-601/192261/20210913140150778_20-601%20Merit%20Reply%20Brief%20Final.pdf","side":"pet","n":34}],"opinion":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/21pdf/595us2r13_n7io.pdf","tr":true,"items":[{"t":"case","label":"Alden v. Maine","cite":"527 U.S. 706","y":1999,"id":"1998/98-436","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"A sharply divided court held in a 5-4 decision that Congress may not use its Article I powers to abrogate the states' sovereign immunity. Both the terms and history of the eleventh amendment suggest that States are immune from suits in their own courts.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"18, 19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Alfred L. Snapp & Son, Inc. v. Puerto Rico, ex rel., Barez","cite":"458 U.S. 592","y":1982,"id":"1981/80-1305","sc":1,"sum":"Puerto Rico has parens patriae standing to maintain this suit. Pp. 600-610. ALFRED L. SNAPP & SON, INC. v. PUERTO RICO (a) In order to maintain a parens patriaeaction, a State must articulate an interest apart from the interests of particular private parties, that is, the State must be more than a nominal party. The State must express a \"quasi-sovereign\" interest, such as its interest in the health and …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"18","2":"12"}},{"t":"case","label":"Am. Pipe & Constr. Co. v. Utah","cite":"414 U.S. 538","y":1974,"id":"1973/72-1195","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that commencing a class action suspends all applicable statutes of limitations against members of that class who would have been parties had the suit been allowed to continue as a class action.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Ams. United for Separation of Church & State v. City of Grand Rapids","cite":"922 F.2d 303","y":1990,"court":"6th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/922/303/","by":{"0":"46"}},{"t":"case","label":"Ariz. State Legislature v. Ariz. Indep. Redistricting Comm’n","cite":"576 U.S. 787","y":2015,"id":"2014/13-1314","sc":1,"sum":"The Court determined that the Elections Clause of the U.S. Constitution does not prohibit a state from creating an independent redistricting commission by initiative.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Arizonans for Off. English v. Arizona","cite":"520 U.S. 43","y":1997,"id":"1996/95-974","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"Announced by Justice Ruth Bader Ginsburg, the Court held that the dispute was moot due to the previous resignation of Yniguez. The Court did not rule on the constitutionality of the article.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"19, 29","1":"12, 40"}},{"t":"case","label":"BNSF Ry. Co. v. E.E.O.C.","cite":"140 S. Ct. 109","y":2019,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.%20Ct./140/109/","sc":1,"by":{"0":"38"}},{"t":"case","label":"Banks v. Chicago Grain Trimmers Ass’n, Inc.","cite":"389 U.S. 813","y":1967,"url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/389/813/","sc":1,"by":{"0":"27"}},{"t":"case","label":"Banks v. Chicago Grain Trimmers Ass’n, Inc.","cite":"390 U.S. 459","y":1968,"id":"1967/59","sc":1,"sum":"Petitioner filed a claim against her late husband's employer for compensation death benefits under the Longshoremen's and Harbor Workers' Compensation Act, alleging that his fall at home on January 30, 1961, from which he later died, resulted from a work-connected injury sustained on January 26. A Department of Labor Deputy Commissioner rejected the claim for failure to establish a work-connected injury. Thereafter, …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"27"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bond v. United States","cite":"564 U.S. 211","y":2011,"id":"2010/09-1227","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that a defendant, convicted under a federal statute, has a right to challenge his/her conviction based on the unconstitutionality of the statute.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Box v. Planned Parenthood of Ind. & Ky., Inc.","cite":"141 S. Ct. 184","y":2020,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.%20Ct./141/184/","sc":1,"by":{"0":"44"}},{"t":"case","label":"Cameron v. EMW Women’s Surgical Ctr., P.S.C.","cite":"141 S. Ct. 1734","y":2021,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.%20Ct./141/1734/","sc":1,"by":{"0":"13"}},{"t":"case","label":"City of Pontiac Retired Emps. Ass’n v. Schimmel","cite":"751 F.3d 427","y":2014,"court":"6th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/751/427/","sum":"Noting the court granted the Michigan Attorney General’s motion to intervene “on behalf of the State of Michigan” at the en banc stage.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"37","1":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Commonwealth ex rel. Beshear v. Commonwealth ex rel. Bevin","cite":"498 S.W.3d 355","y":2016,"court":"Ky.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.W.3d/498/355/","by":{"0":"5"}},{"t":"case","label":"Commonwealth ex rel. Hancock v. Paxton","cite":"516 S.W.2d 865","y":1974,"court":"Ky.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.W.2d/516/865/","op":1,"by":{"0":"4, 5"}},{"t":"case","label":"Day v. Apoliona","cite":"505 F.3d 963","y":2007,"court":"9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/505/963/","op":1,"by":{"0":"passim","1":"21, 26, 31, 39","2":"12, 14, 19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Diamond v. Charles","cite":"476 U.S. 54","y":1986,"id":"1985/84-1379","sc":1,"sum":"Because appellant lacks any judicially cognizable interest in the Illinois Abortion Law, his appeal is dismissed for want of jurisdiction.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"19","2":"12"}},{"t":"case","label":"EMW Women’s Surgical Ctr., P.S.C. v. Friedlander","cite":"978 F.3d 418","y":2020,"court":"6th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/978/418/","by":{"0":"45"}},{"t":"case","label":"Flying J, Inc. v. Van Hollen","cite":"578 F.3d 569","y":2009,"court":"7th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/578/569/","by":{"0":"26, 39, 41"}},{"t":"case","label":"Gonzales v. Carhart","cite":"550 U.S. 124","y":2007,"id":"2006/05-380","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Partial-Birth Abortion Ban Act did not violate a mother's personal liberty guaranteed under the Fifth Amendment.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"7"}},{"t":"case","label":"Gregory v. Ashcroft","cite":"501 U.S. 452","y":1991,"id":"1990/90-50","sc":1,"sum":"In addition to emphasizing Missouri's, or any other state's, Tenth Amendment right to define the qualifications of its highest state officials, the Court held that the regulations of the ADEA did not apply to \"policy-making\" appointees such as state court judges.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Hancock v. Terry Elkhorn Mining Co., Inc.","cite":"503 S.W.2d 710","y":1973,"court":"Ky.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.W.2d/503/710/","by":{"0":"5"}},{"t":"case","label":"Hollingsworth v. Perry","cite":"570 U.S. 693","y":2013,"id":"2012/12-144","sc":1,"ar":3,"sum":"The Court found that the proponents of California's same-sex marriage ban (Proposition 8) do not have standing to appeal the decision that made Proposition 8 unconstitutional.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"3, 19, 20, 45","1":"39","2":"4"}},{"t":"case","label":"Hunter v. Ohio ex rel. Miller","cite":"396 U.S. 879","y":1969,"url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/396/879/","sc":1,"by":{"0":"27"}},{"t":"case","label":"Igartúa v. United States","cite":"636 F.3d 18","y":2011,"court":"1st Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/636/18/","by":{"0":"37"}},{"t":"case","label":"Int’l Union, United Auto., Aerospace & Agric. Implement Workers of Am., AFL-CIO, Local 283 v. Scofield","cite":"382 U.S. 205","y":1965,"id":"1965/18","sc":1,"op":3,"sum":"In No. 18, a union was charged by individual employees with violations of the National Labor Relations Act, and the Board's General Counsel issued a complaint. The NLRB dismissed the complaint after a hearing and the individual employees sought review in the Court of Appeals. The NLRB filed an answer supporting the decision. A motion of intervention filed by the union, although not opposed by the NLRB or the …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"1, 16, 45","1":"22, 23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Izumi Seimitsu Kogyo Kabushiki Kaisha v. U.S. Phillips Corp.","cite":"510 U.S. 27","y":1993,"id":"1993/92-1123","sc":1,"sum":"Petitioner Izumi Seimitsu Kogyo Kabushiki Kaisha was a party at the first trial in an action brought against it and respondent Windmere Corporation by respondent U. S. Philips Corporation, but was not a party to the second trial, in which Windmere prevailed. While the judgments from the second trial were on appeal, respondents reached a settlement and filed a joint motion to vacate the District Court's judgments. …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"1"}},{"t":"case","label":"Johnson v. Commonwealth ex rel. Meredith","cite":"165 S.W.2d 820","y":1942,"court":"Ky.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.W.2d/165/820/","by":{"0":"4"}},{"t":"case","label":"June Med. Servs. L.L.C. v. Russo","cite":"140 S. Ct. 2103","y":2020,"id":"2019/18-1323","sc":1,"op":3,"ar":1,"sum":"A case in which the Court reversed a decision by the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit upholding Louisiana’s law requiring physicians who perform abortions to have admitting privileges at a local hospital because it conflicted with the Court’s binding precedent in Whole Woman’s Health v. Hellerstedt.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"passim","1":"3, 8","2":"21, 22, 23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lopez-Aguilar v. Marion Cnty. Sheriff’s Dep’t","cite":"924 F.3d 375","y":2019,"court":"7th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/924/375/","sum":"Holding that, even if the State’s motion to intervene had been filed earlier, “the burden to the parties of reopening the litigation and resuming settlement negotiations would have been the same”.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"29, 30, 39"}},{"t":"case","label":"Maine v. Taylor","cite":"477 U.S. 131","y":1986,"id":"1985/85-62","sc":1,"op":2,"sum":"In an 8-to-1 decision, the Court held that the limitation imposed by the Commerce Clause on state regulatory power was not absolute and that the States \"retain[ed] authority under their general police powers to regulate matters of 'legitimate local concern.'\" The Court found that Maine's ban on the importation oflive baitfish served a legitimate local purpose that could not adequately be served by available nondiscriminatory alternatives.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"19, 20, 28, 30","2":"11, 13"}},{"t":"case","label":"Maryland v. King","cite":"567 U.S. 1301","y":2012,"url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/567/1301/","sc":1,"by":{"0":"20, 27","2":"11, 17"}},{"t":"case","label":"N. B. D. v. Ky. Cabinet for Health & Family Servs.","cite":"140 S. Ct. 860","y":2020,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.%20Ct./140/860/","sc":1,"op":1,"by":{"0":"38"}},{"t":"case","label":"N.E. Ohio Coal. for Homeless & Serv. Emps. Int’l Union, Local 1199 v. Blackwell","cite":"467 F.3d 999","y":2006,"court":"6th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/467/999/","sum":"Holding that a State has a “manifest legal interest in defending the constitutionality of [its] laws”.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"45"}},{"t":"case","label":"Nat’l Ass’n for the Advancement of Colored People v. New York","cite":"413 U.S. 345","y":1973,"id":"1972/72-129","sc":1,"op":5,"sum":"Sections 4 and 5 of the Voting Rights Act of 1965, as amended, are designed to prohibit the use of tests or devices, or the alteration of voting qualifications or procedures, when the purpose or effect is to deprive a citizen of his right to vote. Sections 4 and 5 apply in any State or political subdivision thereof which the Attorney General determines maintained on November 1, 1964, or November 1, 1968, any \"test …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"17, 36, 41, 42","1":"passim","2":"16, 17, 18, 19"}},{"t":"case","label":"New Motor Vehicle Bd. of Cal. v. Orrin W. Fox Co.","cite":"434 U.S. 1345","y":1977,"url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/434/1345/","sc":1,"by":{"0":"20, 27, 28","2":"11"}},{"t":"case","label":"Nuesse v. Camp","cite":"385 F.2d 694","y":1967,"court":"D.C. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/385/694/","by":{"0":"46"}},{"t":"case","label":"Overstreet v. Mayberry","cite":"603 S.W.3d 244","y":2020,"court":"Ky.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.W.3d/603/244/","by":{"0":"6, 27"}},{"t":"case","label":"Peruta v. Cnty. of San Diego","cite":"824 F.3d 919","y":2016,"court":"9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/824/919/","op":1,"by":{"0":"29, 37","1":"21, 26, 39","2":"14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Planned Parenthood of Tenn. & N. Miss. v. Slatery, --- F. Supp. 3d ---","cite":"2021 WL 765606","y":2021,"court":"M.D. Tenn. Feb. 26","by":{"0":"40"}},{"t":"case","label":"Planned Parenthood of Wis., Inc. v. Kaul","cite":"942 F.3d 793","y":2019,"court":"7th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/942/793/","sum":"Explaining that there is “no reason why a federal court would bat an eye if a state required [one state official] to withdraw from his representation and allow another entity . . . to take over the case”.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"Printz v. United States","cite":"521 U.S. 898","y":1997,"id":"1996/95-1478","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that Congress could not require state agencies to perform federal duties under the Necessary and Proper Clause.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"Pyramid Lake Paiute Tribe of Indians v. Truckee-Carson Irrigation Dist.","cite":"464 U.S. 863","y":1983,"url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/464/863/","sc":1,"by":{"0":"27"}},{"t":"case","label":"Ross v. Marshall","cite":"426 F.3d 745","y":2005,"court":"5th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/426/745/","sum":"Finding post-judgment motion timely where movant provided counsel to the existing party.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"33, 46"}},{"t":"case","label":"Ruthardt v. United States","cite":"303 F.3d 375","y":2002,"court":"1st Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/303/375/","by":{"0":"38"}},{"t":"case","label":"Sec. & Exch. Comm’n v. U.S. Realty & Improvement Co.","cite":"310 U.S. 434","y":1940,"id":"scdb/1939-040","sc":1,"sum":"1. A bankruptcy court has jurisdiction to make orders not subject to collateral attack in a proceeding for an \"arrangement\" with unsecured creditors brought by a debtor corporation under Chapter XI of the Chandler Act, although the financial and corporate setup of the debtor are such that adequate protection and relief can not be obtained under the limitations of that chapter but require a reorganization under …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"46"}},{"t":"case","label":"Smoke v. Norton","cite":"252 F.3d 468","y":2001,"court":"D.C. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/252/468/","by":{"0":"41","1":"41"}},{"t":"case","label":"Trbovich v. United Mine Workers of Am.","cite":"404 U.S. 528","y":1972,"id":"1971/71-119","sc":1,"sum":"Petitioner union member sought unsuccessfully to intervene pur-. suant to Fed. Rule Civ. Proc. 24 (a) in litigation brought by the Secretary of Labor under Title IV of the Labor-Management Reporting and Disclosure Act to set aside an election of union officers for violations of the Act. Petitioner, who initiated the entire enforcement proceeding with his complaint to the Secretary, sought to present evidence and …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"46"}},{"t":"case","label":"United Airlines, Inc. v. McDonald","cite":"432 U.S. 385","y":1977,"id":"1976/76-545","sc":1,"op":4,"sum":"Holding that a motion to intervene was timely because it was made “as soon as it became clear” that the movant's interests “would no longer be protected” by existing parties.","sumSrc":"opinion","by":{"0":"passim","1":"32, 33, 34","2":"11, 16, 19, 20"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. $186,416.00 in U.S. Currency","cite":"722 F.3d 1173","y":2013,"court":"9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/722/1173/","by":{"0":"37, 38"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Detroit","cite":"712 F.3d 925","y":2013,"court":"6th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/712/925/","by":{"0":"36, 41"}},{"t":"case","label":"Va. House of Delegates v. Bethune-Hill","cite":"139 S. Ct. 1945","y":2019,"id":"2018/18-281","sc":1,"sum":"The House of Delegates lacks standing to appeal the invalidation of Virginia’s redistricting plan.","sumSrc":"Holding (supremecourt.gov)","by":{"0":"passim","1":"38, 39, 41","2":"3, 4, 5"}},{"t":"case","label":"W. Pac. R.R. Corp. v. W. Pac. R.R. Co.","cite":"345 U.S. 247","y":1953,"id":"scdb/1952-070","sc":1,"sum":"1. Referring to a United States Court of Appeals, 28 U. S. C. § 46 (c) provides that, \"Cases and controversies shall be heard and determined by a court or division of not more than three judges, unless a hearing or rehearing before the court in banc is ordered by a majority of the circuit judges of the circuit who are in active service.\"","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"43"}},{"t":"case","label":"Whole Woman’s Health v. Hellerstedt","cite":"136 S. Ct. 2292","y":2016,"id":"2015/15-274","sc":1,"sum":"The Court determined that the regulations at issue unconstitutionally imposed an undue burden on women seeking abortions because they created a substantial burden and did not actually serve the stated government interest.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"9"}},{"t":"case","label":"Will v. Mich. Dep’t of State Police","cite":"491 U.S. 58","y":1989,"id":"1988/87-1207","sc":1,"sum":"Neither States nor state officials acting in their official capacities are \"persons\" within meaning of § 1983.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"24","1":"40"}},{"t":"const","label":"Amendment XIV","clauses":[["citizenship-clause","Citizenship Clause"],["privileges-or-immunities","Privileges or Immunities Clause"],["due-process-14","Due Process Clause (Fourteenth Amendment)"],["equal-protection","Equal Protection Clause"],["disqualification","Disqualification Clause (Section 3)"],["enforcement-14","Enforcement Clause (Section 5)"]],"by":{"0":"7"}},{"t":"usc","label":"28 U.S.C. § 1254","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1254","subs":["(1)"],"by":{"0":"1"}},{"t":"usc","label":"28 U.S.C. § 1331","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1331","by":{"0":"7"}},{"t":"usc","label":"28 U.S.C. § 1343","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1343","by":{"0":"7"}},{"t":"usc","label":"28 U.S.C. § 2106","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/2106","by":{"0":"1"}},{"t":"rule","label":"Fed. R. App. P. 35","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frap/rule_35","subs":["(c)"],"by":{"0":"11, 31","1":"31"}},{"t":"rule","label":"Fed. R. App. P. 40","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frap/rule_40","subs":["(a)(1)"],"by":{"0":"11, 31"}},{"t":"rule","label":"Fed. R. App. P. 41","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frap/rule_41","subs":["(b)"],"by":{"0":"31"}},{"t":"rule","label":"Fed. R. App. P. 42","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frap/rule_42","subs":["(b)"],"by":{"0":"34"}},{"t":"rule","label":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 1","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcp/rule_1","by":{"0":"16"}},{"t":"rule","label":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 24","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcp/rule_24","subs":["(a)"],"by":{"0":"16, 17, 33, 36, 45, 45"}},{"t":"case","label":"AAL High Yield Bond Fund v. Deloitte & Touche LLP","cite":"361 F.3d 1305","y":2004,"court":"11th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/361/1305/","by":{"1":"17, 18"}},{"t":"case","label":"ACLU v. United States","cite":"538 U.S. 920","y":2003,"url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/538/920/","sc":1,"by":{"1":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"Alaska State Legislature v. United States","cite":"534 U.S. 1038","y":2001,"url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/534/1038/","sc":1,"by":{"1":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"Alleghany Corp. v. Kirby","cite":"344 F.2d 571","y":1965,"court":"2d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/344/571/","by":{"1":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Am. Forest & Paper Ass’n v. League of Wilderness Defs./Blue Mountains Biodiversity Project","cite":"540 U.S. 805","y":2003,"url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/540/805/","sc":1,"by":{"1":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"Amador County v. U.S. Dep’t of Interior","cite":"772 F.3d 901","y":2014,"court":"D.C. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/772/901/","ar":1,"sum":"Rejecting argument that denial of intervention was abuse of discretion because it “undervalued” sovereign interests and holding “[w]e have never held that a district court must give extra weight or special consideration to a sovereign’s purpose for intervention”.","sumSrc":"brief:1","by":{"1":"22, 24, 28","2":"12"}},{"t":"case","label":"Amalgamated Transit Union Int’l, AFL-CIO v. Donovan","cite":"771 F.2d 1551","y":1985,"court":"D.C. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/771/1551/","op":1,"by":{"1":"23, 25"}},{"t":"case","label":"Amgen Inc. v. Connecticut Ret. Plans & Tr. Funds","cite":"568 U.S. 455","y":2013,"id":"2012/11-1085","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that during class certification, the court does not need proof of materiality and does not have to allow the defendant to present evidence to rebut the certification.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"43"}},{"t":"case","label":"Arbaugh v. Y&H Corp.","cite":"546 U.S. 500","y":2006,"id":"2005/04-944","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court held that the number-of-employees requirement is an element of the merits of a Title VII claim, not a jurisdictional limitation.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bloom v. F.D.I.C.","cite":"738 F.3d 58","y":2013,"court":"2d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/738/58/","by":{"1":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Board of Regents of Univ. of Wis. System v. Southworth","cite":"529 U.S. 217","y":2000,"id":"1999/98-1189","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court held that the \"First Amendment permits a public university to charge its students an activity fee used to fund a program to facilitate extracurricular student speech if the program is viewpoint neutral.\" Justice Kennedy wrote for the Court that, \"[w]hen a university requires its students to pay fees to support the extracurricular speech of other students, all in the interest of open discussion, it may not prefer some viewpoints to others.\" Justice David H. Souter, in an opinion joined by Justices John Paul Stevens and Stephen G. Breyer, concurred in the judgment only.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"43"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bond v. Utreras","cite":"585 F.3d 1061","y":2009,"court":"7th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/585/1061/","by":{"1":"33"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bowles v. Russell","cite":"551 U.S. 205","y":2007,"id":"2006/06-5306","sc":1,"op":2,"sum":"The Court ruled that, even though Bowles was relying on the mistaken order of the District Court, the Circuit Court was correct to dismiss his untimely appeal.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"passim"}},{"t":"case","label":"California v. Texas","cite":"459 U.S. 1096","y":1983,"url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/459/1096/","sc":1,"by":{"1":"15, 16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Christian Legal Soc’y Chapter of Univ. of Cal., Hastings Coll. of Law v. Martinez","cite":"561 U.S. 661","y":2010,"id":"2009/08-1371","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court held that a college's mandate requiring all officially-recognized student organizations be inclusive and welcoming to all students did not violate a group of Christian students' First Amendment rights when the college refused to recognize a student group that required all members to sign a statement of Christian beliefs for membership.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"43, 45, 46"}},{"t":"case","label":"City of New Orleans v. Citizens’ Bank of La.","cite":"167 U.S. 371","y":1897,"id":"scdb/1896-206","sc":1,"sum":"By the act of January 30, 1836, the.legislature of Louisiana exempted the capital of the Citizens’ Bank in New Orleans from taxation.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Cooter & Gell v. Hartmarx Corp.","cite":"496 U.S. 384","y":1990,"id":"1989/89-275","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"Respondents, the defendants in a District Court suit instituted by petitioner law firm on behalf of a client, filed a motion to dismiss the complaint as having no basis in fact and a motion for sanctions under Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 11 on the ground that the firm had not made sufficient prefiling inquiries to support the complaint's allegations. Rule 11-after specifying, interalia, that an attorney's …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"25, 32"}},{"t":"case","label":"Craig v. Simon","cite":"980 F.3d 614","y":2020,"court":"8th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/980/614/","by":{"1":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Davis v. Wakelee","cite":"156 U.S. 680","y":1895,"id":"scdb/1894-130","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"An appeal authorized by the appellant personally,' and in good faith entered in this court in the name of his attorney and counsel below, will not be dismissed simply because that counsel had not authorized such entry, when the appellant, on learning of the mistake, appears by other counsel and prosecutes it in good faith.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"43"}},{"t":"case","label":"Devlin v. Scardelletti","cite":"536 U.S. 1","y":2002,"id":"2001/01-417","sc":1,"op":17,"ar":1,"sum":"The Court held that nonnamed class members like Devlin, who have objected in a timely manner to approval of the settlement at the fairness hearing, have the power to bring an appeal without first intervening.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"17","2":"7, 8, 9"}},{"t":"case","label":"EEOC v. Catastrophe Mgmt. Sols.","cite":"138 S. Ct. 2015","y":2018,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.%20Ct./138/2015/","sc":1,"by":{"1":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"EEOC v. R.G. & G.R. Harris Funeral Homes, Inc.","cite":"2017 WL 10350992","y":2017,"court":"6th Cir. Mar. 27","by":{"1":"28, 34"}},{"t":"case","label":"EMW Women’s Surgical Ctr., P.S.C. v. Beshear","cite":"920 F.3d 421","y":2019,"court":"6th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/920/421/","by":{"1":"28"}},{"t":"case","label":"Farmland Dairies v. Comm’r of N.Y. State Dep’t of Agric. & Markets","cite":"847 F.2d 1038","y":1988,"court":"2d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/847/1038/","by":{"1":"33"}},{"t":"case","label":"Federated Dep’t Stores, Inc. v. Moitie","cite":"452 U.S. 394","y":1981,"id":"1980/79-1517","sc":1,"sum":"Res judicata bars relitigation of the unappealed adverse judgments against respondents as to their federal-law claims. The res judicata consequences of a final, unappealed judgment on the merits are not altered by the fact that the judgment may have been wrong or rested on a legal principle subsequently overruled in another case. There is no general equitable doctrine which countenances an exception to the finality …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Fiore v. White","cite":"528 U.S. 23","y":1999,"url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/528/23/","sc":1,"by":{"1":"39"}},{"t":"case","label":"Garrity v. Gallen","cite":"697 F.2d 452","y":1983,"court":"1st Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/697/452/","by":{"1":"33"}},{"t":"case","label":"Gary B. v. Whitmer","cite":"958 F.3d 1216","y":2020,"court":"6th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/958/1216/","by":{"1":"10"}},{"t":"case","label":"Gen. Elec. Co. v. Joiner","cite":"522 U.S. 136","y":1997,"id":"1997/96-188","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that abuse of discretion, the standard ordinarily applicable to review of evidentiary rulings, is the proper standard by which to review a district court's decision to admit or exclude expert scientific evidence.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"22"}},{"t":"case","label":"Georgia v. Ashcroft","cite":"539 U.S. 461","y":2003,"id":"2002/02-182","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the District Court failed to consider all the relevant factors when it examined whether Georgia's Senate plan resulted in a retrogression of black voters' effective exercise of the electoral franchise.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"37"}},{"t":"case","label":"Gonzales v. Thaler","cite":"565 U.S. 134","y":2012,"id":"2011/10-895","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court held that a prisoner who does not seek review in a state’s highest court faces final judgment for the purposes of Section 2244(d)(1)(A) upon “expiration of the time for seeking such review.”","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"14, 16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Griggs v. Provident Consumer Disc. Co.","cite":"459 U.S. 56","y":1982,"id":"1982/82-5082","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court of Appeals' analysis of Rule 4(a)(4) is contrary to the language and purposes of the 1979 amendments to the Rules of Appellate Procedure. Prior to 1979, if a notice of appeal was fied pending disposition of a motion to vacate, alter, or amend the judgment it was generally held that the district court retained jurisdiction to decide the motion and the notice of appeal was adequate for purposes of beginning …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Hall v. Holder","cite":"117 F.3d 1222","y":1997,"court":"11th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/117/1222/","by":{"1":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Hamer v. Neighborhood Hous. Servs. of Chi.","cite":"138 S. Ct. 13","y":2017,"id":"2017/16-658","sc":1,"sum":"A case in which Court held that Federal Rule of Appellate Procedure 4(a)(5)(C), which places a time limit on appeals, is not jurisdictional because it is a court-made rule rather than a statute.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Hodges, Grant & Kaufmann v. U.S. Gov’t, Dep’t of Treasury, I.R.S.","cite":"762 F.2d 1299","y":1985,"court":"5th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/762/1299/","by":{"1":"33"}},{"t":"case","label":"Horne v. Flores","cite":"557 U.S. 433","y":2009,"id":"2008/08-289","sc":1,"sum":"The Court found that the lower court incorrectly analyzed Rule 60(b)(5), which allows a party to seek relief from a judgment if a significant change in the law has occurred.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"36, 37","2":"22"}},{"t":"case","label":"Hutchison v. Pfeil","cite":"211 F.3d 515","y":2000,"court":"10th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/211/515/","op":1,"by":{"1":"19, 20, 23"}},{"t":"case","label":"JPMorgan Chase & Co. v. Fed. Hous. Fin. Agency","cite":"134 S. Ct. 372","y":2013,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.%20Ct./134/372/","sc":1,"by":{"1":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"Kontrick v. Ryan","cite":"540 U.S. 443","y":2004,"id":"2003/02-819","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that Kontrick lost the right to invoke Rule 4004's time limitation because he did so after the bankruptcy court reached the merits of Ryan's objection. The Court rejected Kontrick's claim that Rule 4004 is \"jurisdictional\" and that it trumps other considerations whenever raised in proceedings.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"Koon v. United States","cite":"518 U.S. 81","y":1996,"id":"1995/94-1664","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Court of Appeals should not review de novo a decision to depart from the Guideline sentencing range, but instead should ask whether the sentencing court abused its discretion.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"24"}},{"t":"case","label":"Landreth Timber Co. v. Landreth","cite":"731 F.2d 1348","y":1984,"court":"9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/731/1348/","by":{"1":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lapides v. Board of Regents of Univ. Sys. of Ga.","cite":"535 U.S. 613","y":2002,"id":"2001/01-298","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that a State waives its Eleventh Amendment immunity when it removes a case from state court to federal court.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"45"}},{"t":"case","label":"League of United Latin Am. Citizens v. Clements","cite":"999 F.2d 831","y":1993,"court":"5th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/999/831/","sum":"Rejecting Texas Attorney General’s “remarkable” contention that “the Attorney General is the sole arbiter of State policy when the State’s interest is in litigation,” especially where “[t]he state courts have had little occasion to face such a bold claim of authority”.","sumSrc":"brief:1","by":{"1":"39"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lehman Bros. v. Schein","cite":"416 U.S. 386","y":1974,"id":"1973/73-439","sc":1,"sum":"While resort to an !,xailable certification procedure, such as is available in Florida, is not-obligatory where there is doubt as to local law, and its use in a given case is discretionary, resort to such procedure seems particularly appropriate here in view of the novelty of the question, the unsettled state of Florida law, and the fact that when federal judges in New York attempt to predict uncertain Florida law, …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"39"}},{"t":"case","label":"Marino v. Ortiz","cite":"484 U.S. 301","y":1988,"id":"1987/86-1415","sc":1,"op":2,"sum":"The New York City Police Department and groups representing various of its minority officers reached a settlement of the groups' employment discrimination suit under Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, which settlement was first approved by the District Court on an interim basis and, finally, after a hearing, by consent decree. Although petitionersa group of white officers claiming to be adversely affected by …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"18","2":"5, 6, 7"}},{"t":"case","label":"Martin v. Wilkes","cite":"490 U.S. 755","y":1989,"id":"1988/87-1614","sc":1,"sum":"In a 5-to-4 decision, the Court held that because \"a person cannot be deprived of his legal rights in a proceeding to which he is not a party,\" the white firefighters were not precluded from challenging employment decisions taken pursuant to the consent decrees.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Melendres v. Maricopa County","cite":"815 F.3d 645","y":2016,"court":"9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/815/645/","by":{"1":"16, 19","2":"9"}},{"t":"case","label":"Microsoft Corp. v. Baker","cite":"137 S. Ct. 1702","y":2017,"id":"2016/15-457","sc":1,"sum":"The Court determined that the federal courts of appeals lack jurisdiction to review an order denying class certification after the named plaintiffs have voluntarily dismissed their claims with prejudice.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"17, 21"}},{"t":"case","label":"Minerva Surgical, Inc. v. Hologic, Inc.","cite":"141 S. Ct. 2298","y":2021,"id":"2020/20-440","sc":1,"sum":"The well-grounded patent law doctrine of assignor estoppel applies only when the assignor’s claim of invalidity contradicts explicit or implicit representations the assignor made in assigning the patent.","sumSrc":"Holding (supremecourt.gov)","by":{"1":"43"}},{"t":"case","label":"Missouri v. Jenkins","cite":"495 U.S. 33","y":1990,"id":"1989/88-1150","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the District Court \"abused its discretion\" by imposing a specific tax increase.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"15"}},{"t":"case","label":"Morales Feliciano v. Rullan","cite":"303 F.3d 1","y":2002,"court":"1st Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/303/1/","op":1,"by":{"1":"16, 45"}},{"t":"case","label":"Morin v. City of Stuart","cite":"112 F.2d 585","y":1939,"court":"5th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/112/585/","by":{"1":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"N.Y. Petroleum Corp. v. Ashland Oil, Inc.","cite":"757 F.2d 288","y":1985,"court":"Temp. Emer. Ct. App.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/757/288/","by":{"1":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"NCAA v. Keller","cite":"134 S. Ct. 980","y":2014,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.%20Ct./134/980/","sc":1,"by":{"1":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"New Hampshire v. Maine","cite":"532 U.S. 742","y":2001,"id":"2000/130_orig","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court held that \"judicial estoppel bars New Hampshire from asserting that the Piscataqua River boundary runs along the Maine shore.\" Under the judicial estoppel doctrine, \"[w]here a party assumes a certain position in a legal proceeding, and succeeds in maintaining that position, he may not thereafter, simply because his interests have changed, assume a contrary position, especially if it be to the prejudice of the party who has acquiesced in the position formerly taken by him,\" wrote Justice Ginsburg. \"New Hampshire's claim that the Piscataqua River boundary runs along the Maine shore is clearly inconsistent with its interpretation of the words 'Middle of the River' during the 1970's litigation,\" concluded Ginsburg.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"passim","2":"24"}},{"t":"case","label":"NHL v. Metro. Hockey Club, Inc.","cite":"427 U.S. 639","y":1976,"id":"1975/75-1558","sc":1,"sum":"District Court did not abuse its discretion in resorting to the extreme sanction of dismissing respondents' antitrust action against petitioners pursuant to Fed. Rule Civ. Proc. 37, the court having found that, notwithstanding its admonitions and numerous extensions, respondents failed to respond to written interrogatories ordered by the court, and that under the circumstances such failure manifested \"flagrant bad …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"22, 27"}},{"t":"case","label":"Ohio v. Foust","cite":"565 U.S. 1233","y":2012,"url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/565/1233/","sc":1,"sum":"Explaining amended rule will sustain appeal from unnamed party only if “it is objectively clear [from notice of appeal] that a party intended to appeal”.","sumSrc":"brief:1","by":{"1":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"Pediatric Specialty Care, Inc. v. Ark. Dep’t of Hum. Servs.","cite":"364 F.3d 925","y":2004,"court":"8th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/364/925/","by":{"1":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Pegram v. Herdrich","cite":"530 U.S. 211","y":2000,"id":"1999/98-1949","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that mixed treatment and eligibility decisions to delay medical treatment by sending a patient to a HMO owned facility, with adverse consequences, made by a health maintenance organization through its physician, are not fiduciary decisions under ERISA.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"43, 45"}},{"t":"case","label":"Richardson v. Flores","cite":"979 F.3d 1102","y":2020,"court":"5th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/979/1102/","sum":"Rejecting argument that same standards are applied to district court and appellate intervention motions.","sumSrc":"brief:1","by":{"1":"22, 23, 33"}},{"t":"case","label":"Rufo v. Inmates of Suffolk Cnty. Jail","cite":"502 U.S. 367","y":1992,"id":"1991/90-954","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the court does not require a grievous wrong evoked by new and unforeseen change of facts in order to modify a consented decree.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"37","2":"22"}},{"t":"case","label":"S.E.C. v. Dunlap","cite":"253 F.3d 768","y":2001,"court":"4th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/253/768/","by":{"1":"19, 20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Schlup v. Delo","cite":"513 U.S. 298","y":1995,"id":"1994/93-7901","sc":1,"sum":"Petitioner Schlup, a Missouri prisoner, was convicted of participating in the murder of a fellow inmate and sentenced to death. In this, his second federal habeas petition, he alleged that constitutional error at his trial deprived the jury of critical evidence that would have established his innocence. The District Court declined to reach the petition's merits, holding that Schlup could not satisfy the threshold …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"37"}},{"t":"case","label":"Semtek Int’l Inc. v. Lockheed Martin Corp.","cite":"531 U.S. 497","y":2001,"id":"2000/99-1551","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that \"[b]ecause the claim-preclusive effect of a federal court's dismissal 'upon the merits' of a diversity action on state statute-of-limitations grounds is governed by a federal rule, which in turn (in diversity cases) incorporates the claim-preclusion law that would be applied by state courts in the State in which the federal court sits, the Maryland Court of Special Appeals erred in holding that the California federal court's dismissal 'upon the merits' necessarily precluded the Maryland state-court action.\" Thus, the District Court's dismissal on the merits of claims as untimely under California law did not preclude Semtek from asserting its claims in a different state court forum.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"46"}},{"t":"case","label":"Sorrell v. IMS Health Inc.","cite":"564 U.S. 552","y":2011,"id":"2010/10-779","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court held that a Vermont statute banning the sale, transmission or use of prescription drug user data violated protected speech of the pharmaceutical research companies, manufacturers and others who use that data.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"43"}},{"t":"case","label":"Spring Constr. Co. v. Harris","cite":"614 F.2d 374","y":1980,"court":"4th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/614/374/","by":{"1":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Taylor v. Sturgell","cite":"553 U.S. 880","y":2008,"id":"2007/07-371","sc":1,"op":2,"ar":1,"sum":"Writing for a unanimous Court, Justice Ruth Bader Ginsburg held that such \"nonparty preclusion\" runs up against the \"deep-rooted historic tradition that everyone should have his own day in court.\" Virtual representation should only be applied rarely and under certain exceptions to the general rule, none of which the Court found applicable in this case.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"15","2":"7"}},{"t":"case","label":"Torres v. Oakland Scavenger Co.","cite":"487 U.S. 312","y":1988,"id":"1987/86-1845","sc":1,"op":4,"sum":"Failure to file a notice of appeal in accordance with Federal Rule of Appellate Procedure 3(c)'s requirement that the notice \"specify the party or parties taking the appeal\" presents a jurisdictional bar to the appeal. The Rule's caveat that an appeal \"shall not be dismissed for [the notice's] informality of form or title\" does not aid petitioner, since his exclusion from the notice constitutes a failure to appeal …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"passim"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States House of Representatives v. Price","cite":"2017 WL 3271445","y":2017,"court":"D.C. Cir. Aug. 1","by":{"1":"28"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. County of Maricopa","cite":"889 F.3d 648","y":2018,"court":"9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/889/648/","sum":"Holding that, by jointly moving for dismissal under Rule 41, former defendant “agreed to delegate responsibility for defense of the action to [remaining parties], knowing that it could be bound by the judgment later despite its formal absence as a party”.","sumSrc":"brief:1","by":{"1":"18, 38"}},{"t":"case","label":"Utah v. United States","cite":"394 U.S. 89","y":1969,"id":"1968/31_orig","sc":1,"sum":"The Special Master's Report will be placed on file and his denial of intervention is approved, since a Stipulation entered into between Utah and the United States has so limited the issues that the presence of Morton and other private claimants is neither necessary nor appropriate. Vernon B. Romney, Attorney General of Utah, Robert B. Hansen, Deputy Attorney General, and Dallin W. Jensen, Assistant Attorney General, …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"22"}},{"t":"case","label":"Virginia Off. for Prot. & Advoc. v. Stewart","cite":"563 U.S. 247","y":2011,"id":"2010/09-529","sc":1,"op":1,"ar":5,"sum":"The Court held that a federal court may hear a lawsuit for prospective injunctive relief against state officials brought by another agency of the same state.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"40"}},{"t":"case","label":"Wisconsin Dep’t of Corr. v. Schacht","cite":"524 U.S. 381","y":1998,"id":"1997/97-461","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that a State's claim of an Eleventh Amendment bar to a plaintiff's claims against the state places those claims beyond the power of the federal courts to decide, but that the State may remove the case to a federal court and that the court can decide the nonbarred claims. \"A State's proper assertion of an Eleventh Amendment bar after removal means that the federal court cannot hear the barred claim.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"47"}},{"t":"case","label":"Yniguez v. Arizona","cite":"939 F.2d 727","y":1991,"court":"9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/939/727/","op":1,"by":{"1":"passim"}},{"t":"case","label":"York v. Fed. Bureau of Prisons","cite":"379 F. App’x 737","y":2010,"court":"10th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.%20App%E2%80%99x/379/737/","by":{"1":"23"}},{"t":"usc","label":"28 U.S.C. § 2107","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/2107","stext":{"head":"§2107. Time for appeal to court of appeals","paras":[[0,"(a) Except as otherwise provided in this section, no appeal shall bring any judgment, order or decree in an action, suit or proceeding of a civil nature before a court of appeals for review unless notice of appeal is filed, within thirty days after the entry of such judgment, order or decree."],[0,"(b) In any such action, suit, or proceeding, the time as to all parties shall be 60 days from such entry if one of the parties is—"],[1,"(1) the United States;"],[1,"(2) a United States agency;"],[1,"(3) a United States officer or employee sued in an official capacity; or"],[1,"(4) a current or former United States officer or employee sued in an individual capacity for an act or omission occurring in connection with duties performed on behalf of the United States, including all instances in which the United States represents that officer or employee when the judgment, order, or decree is entered or files the appeal for that officer or employee."],[0,"(c) The district court may, upon motion filed not later than 30 days after the expiration of the time otherwise set for bringing appeal, extend the time for appeal upon a showing of excusable neglect or good cause. In addition, if the district court finds—"],[1,"(1) that a party entitled to notice of the entry of a judgment or order did not receive such notice from the clerk or any party within 21 days of its entry, and"],[1,"(2) that no party would be prejudiced,"],[0,"the district court may, upon motion filed within 180 days after entry of the judgment or order or within 14 days after receipt of such notice, whichever is earlier, reopen the time for appeal for a period of 14 days from the date of entry of the order reopening the time for appeal."],[0,"(d) This section shall not apply to bankruptcy matters or other proceedings under Title 11."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/28/2107?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":4,"ar":1,"head":"§2107. Time for appeal to court of appeals","tname":"Judiciary and Judicial Procedure","by":{"1":"1, 11, 26"}},{"t":"usc","label":"42 U.S.C. § 1983","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/1983","by":{"1":"3, 12"}},{"t":"rule","label":"Fed. R. App. P. 3","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frap/rule_3","by":{"1":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Acree v. Republic of Iraq","cite":"370 F.3d 41","y":2004,"court":"D.C. Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/370/41/","by":{"2":"11"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bender v. Williamsport Area Sch. Dist.","cite":"475 U.S. 534","y":1986,"id":"1985/84-773","sc":1,"op":1,"ar":1,"sum":"Respondent had no standing to appeal, and therefore the Court of Appeals had no jurisdiction to hear his appeal.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"3"}},{"t":"case","label":"Brnovich v. Democratic Nat’l Comm.","cite":"141 S. Ct. 2321","y":2021,"id":"2020/19-1257","sc":1,"ar":1,"sum":"Arizona’s challenged voting regulations governing precinct-based election-day voting (rejecting ballots cast in the wrong precinct) and early mail-in voting (making it a crime for anyone other than an authorized proxy to possess the early ballot of another voter) do not violate §2 of the Voting Rights Act of 1965; Arizona House Bill 2023 (enacting the early mail-in voting regulations) was not enacted with a racially discriminatory purpose.","sumSrc":"Holding (supremecourt.gov)","by":{"2":"4, 13, 14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Cameron v. Beshear, --- S.W.3d ---","cite":"2021 WL 3730708","y":2021,"court":"Ky. Aug. 21","by":{"2":"5"}},{"t":"case","label":"Heckler v. Cmty. Health Servs. of Crawford Cnty., Inc.","cite":"467 U.S. 51","y":1984,"id":"1983/83-56","sc":1,"sum":"Under the Medicare program, providers of health care services are reimbursed for the reasonable cost of services rendered to Medicare beneficiaries and are required to submit annual cost reports which are audited to determine actual costs. The Secretary of Health and Human Services (Secretary) may reopen any reimbursement determination within a 3-year period and make appropriate adjustments. Respondent nonprofit …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"24"}},{"t":"case","label":"Hispanic Soc’y of N.Y. City Police Dep’t Inc. v. N.Y. City Police Dep’t","cite":"806 F.2d 1147","y":1986,"court":"2d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/806/1147/","by":{"2":"7"}},{"t":"case","label":"Karcher v. May","cite":"484 U.S. 72","y":1987,"id":"1987/85-1551","sc":1,"op":2,"ar":1,"sum":"The Court held that once the individuals left their offices, the authority to pursue the lawsuit on behalf of the Legislature passed to their successors. Because Karcher and Orechio’s successors withdrew the appeal, there was no longer an active case or controversy, so the Court had no jurisdiction to hear the case under Article III.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"3"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lawrence on behalf of Lawrence v. Chater","cite":"516 U.S. 163","y":1996,"id":"1995/94-9323","sc":1,"sum":"In a 7-2 per curiam opinion, the Court held that it had the power to issue a GVR order and that such an order is an appropriate exercise of its discretionary certiorari jurisdiction. \"In an appropriate case, a GVR order conserves the scarce resources of this Court that might otherwise be expended on plenary consideration, assists the court below by flagging a particular issue that it does not appear to have fully considered, assists this Court by procuring the benefit of the lower court's insight before we rule on the merits, and alleviates the 'potential for unequal treatment' that is inherent in our inability to grant plenary review of all pending cases raising similar issues,\" stated the unsigned opinion.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Reed Elsevier, Inc. v. Muchnick","cite":"559 U.S. 154","y":2010,"id":"2009/08-103","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the registration requirement of Section 411(a) of the Copyright Act is a precondition to filing a copyright infringement claim.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"24"}},{"t":"case","label":"Republic of Iraq v. Beaty","cite":"556 U.S. 848","y":2009,"id":"2008/07-1090","sc":1,"op":7,"by":{"2":"11"}},{"t":"case","label":"U.S. ex rel. Eisenstein v. City of N.Y.","cite":"556 U.S. 928","y":2009,"id":"2008/08-660","sc":1,"op":8,"ar":2,"sum":"The Court held that the 30-day limit for filing a claim under the False Claims Act applies when the United States is not a party to the lawsuit.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"5, 7, 8"}},{"t":"case","label":"Whole Woman’s Health v. Paxton, --- F.4th ---","cite":"2021 WL 3661318","y":2021,"court":"5th Cir. Aug. 18","by":{"2":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Zenith Radio Corp. v. Hazeltine Rsch., Inc.","cite":"395 U.S. 100","y":1969,"id":"1968/49","sc":1,"sum":"Upon the expiration in 1959 of petitioner, Zenith's, license agreement with Hazeltine Research, Inc. (HRI), which permitted Zenith to use all of HRI's domestic radio and television patents under HRI's so-called standard package license, Zenith refused to renew, asserting that it no longer required a license. HRI brought a patent infringement suit in November 1959. Zenith's answer alleged invalidity of the patent, …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"6"}},{"t":"rule","label":"Fed. R. Civ. P. 60","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/frcp/rule_60","subs":["(b)(5)"],"by":{"2":"6, 21, 22, 23"}}]};